## 3. Availability

**What it means:** The percentage of time the system is operational and accessible to users.

**Key Concepts:**

- **Uptime** — Percentage of time system is available. 99.9% (three nines) allows ~8.7 hours downtime per year. 99.99% (four nines) allows ~52 minutes per year.

- **Redundancy** — Duplicate components that take over when primary fails. Active-active (all serving traffic) or active-passive (standby waiting).

- **Failover** — Automatic switching to a backup system when primary fails.

- **Single Point of Failure (SPOF)** — Any component whose failure brings down the entire system. Must be eliminated.

- **Planned vs Unplanned Downtime** — Scheduled maintenance vs unexpected outages. Both count against availability.

**What needs to be done:**

- Identify and eliminate single points of failure
- Deploy across multiple availability zones
- Set up redundant databases with automatic failover
- Configure load balancers with health checks
- Implement zero-downtime deployment strategies (blue-green, canary, rolling)
- Define maintenance windows and communication procedures
- Set up redundant network paths
- Use multiple DNS providers or DNS failover
- Monitor availability continuously with synthetic checks

**QA Automation:**

- Set up automated health check endpoints and monitor them continuously
- Implement synthetic monitoring from multiple geographic locations
- Automate failover testing (kill primary, verify secondary takes over)
- Create automated tests for zero-downtime deployments
- Test load balancer failover scenarios automatically
- Automate DNS failover validation
- Implement automated canary deployment verification
- Create chaos tests that randomly terminate instances
- Automate verification of redundancy (kill components, verify system stays up)
- Set up automated SLA tracking and reporting
- Test maintenance mode functionality automatically

