## 6. Security

**What it means:** Protecting the system, data, and users from unauthorized access, breaches, and attacks.

**Key Concepts:**

- **Authentication** — Verifying identity (who are you?). Methods include passwords, MFA, OAuth, SAML, certificates.

- **Authorization** — Verifying permissions (what can you do?). Implemented via RBAC (role-based) or ABAC (attribute-based).

- **Encryption at Rest** — Protecting stored data using encryption (database, files, backups).

- **Encryption in Transit** — Protecting data moving between systems using TLS/HTTPS.

- **Principle of Least Privilege** — Granting minimum permissions necessary to perform a function.

- **Defense in Depth** — Multiple layers of security controls so breaching one doesn't compromise the system.

- **Zero Trust** — Never trust, always verify. Authenticate and authorize every request regardless of source.

**What needs to be done:**

- Implement strong authentication (MFA for sensitive operations)
- Design authorization model (RBAC/ABAC)
- Encrypt all data at rest and in transit
- Implement secure secret management (no secrets in code)
- Set up Web Application Firewall (WAF)
- Configure network segmentation and firewalls
- Implement input validation and output encoding
- Protect against OWASP Top 10 vulnerabilities
- Set up security headers (CSP, HSTS, X-Frame-Options)
- Conduct regular vulnerability scanning and penetration testing
- Implement API security (rate limiting, authentication, validation)
- Set up security monitoring and alerting
- Define incident response procedures
- Train developers on secure coding practices

**QA Automation:**

- Integrate SAST (Static Application Security Testing) in CI pipeline (SonarQube, Checkmarx, Semgrep)
- Implement DAST (Dynamic Application Security Testing) automation (OWASP ZAP, Burp Suite)
- Automate dependency vulnerability scanning (Snyk, Dependabot, npm audit)
- Create automated tests for authentication flows (login, logout, session expiry, MFA)
- Automate authorization testing (verify users can't access unauthorized resources)
- Implement automated SQL injection and XSS testing
- Create automated tests for security headers validation
- Automate SSL/TLS certificate validation and expiry monitoring
- Implement secrets scanning in code repositories (GitLeaks, TruffleHog)
- Automate penetration testing for common vulnerabilities
- Create automated tests for rate limiting and brute force protection
- Implement container security scanning (Trivy, Clair)
- Automate API security testing (authentication, input validation, output encoding)
- Set up automated compliance scanning against security benchmarks (CIS)

