## 7. Maintainability

**What it means:** How easily the system can be modified, updated, debugged, and supported over time.

**Key Concepts:**

- **Code Quality** — Readable, well-structured, documented code that follows consistent standards.

- **Technical Debt** — Shortcuts or quick fixes that make future changes harder. Must be tracked and managed.

- **Modularity** — Breaking system into independent, interchangeable components with clear interfaces.

- **Testability** — How easily the system can be tested. High testability means faster, more confident changes.

- **Documentation** — Clear explanations of architecture, APIs, processes, and decisions.

- **Configuration Management** — Externalizing configuration from code so changes don't require redeployment.

**What needs to be done:**

- Establish and enforce coding standards
- Implement automated code review and linting
- Maintain high test coverage (unit, integration, e2e)
- Set up CI/CD pipelines for automated testing and deployment
- Use infrastructure as code (Terraform, CloudFormation)
- Externalize configuration using environment variables or config services
- Create and maintain API documentation
- Document architecture decisions (ADRs)
- Implement feature flags for safer releases
- Create runbooks for operational procedures
- Track and address technical debt regularly
- Keep dependencies updated

**QA Automation:**

- Implement automated code quality gates (fail builds below coverage threshold)
- Set up automated linting and code style enforcement
- Automate test coverage reporting and trend tracking
- Create automated dependency update PRs (Dependabot, Renovate)
- Implement automated API contract testing (Pact, Dredd)
- Automate documentation generation and validation
- Create automated tests for database migrations (run migration, verify schema)
- Implement automated code complexity analysis
- Set up automated technical debt tracking
- Automate feature flag cleanup validation
- Create automated tests for configuration changes
- Implement automated rollback testing
- Set up automated changelog generation
- Create smoke tests that run after every deployment

