## 9. Compatibility

**What it means:** The system's ability to work correctly with other systems, platforms, browsers, and data formats.

**Key Concepts:**

- **Browser Compatibility** — Supporting different browsers and versions (Chrome, Firefox, Safari, Edge).

- **API Versioning** — Managing changes to APIs without breaking existing consumers. Common approaches: URL versioning (/v1/), header versioning.

- **Backward Compatibility** — New versions continue to work with older clients or data.

- **Forward Compatibility** — Older versions can handle newer data formats gracefully (ignoring unknown fields).

- **Interoperability** — Ability to exchange and use information with other systems.

**What needs to be done:**

- Define supported browsers and versions
- Define supported devices and operating systems
- Implement API versioning strategy from the start
- Document all APIs (OpenAPI/Swagger)
- Define data format standards (JSON schema)
- Plan deprecation policy for old API versions
- Implement contract testing for integrations
- Handle unknown fields gracefully in APIs
- Test across all supported platforms
- Document integration requirements for third parties

**QA Automation:**

- Implement API contract testing (Pact) to catch breaking changes
- Automate backward compatibility testing (test new version with old clients)
- Create automated tests for API versioning (verify old versions still work)
- Implement automated schema validation for API requests/responses
- Set up cross-browser automated testing matrix
- Automate cross-platform testing (Windows, Mac, Linux, mobile)
- Create automated tests for data format compatibility (JSON, XML, CSV)
- Implement automated integration tests with third-party systems
- Automate API deprecation warnings verification
- Create automated tests for graceful handling of unknown fields
- Implement consumer-driven contract testing
- Automate database backward compatibility testing for migrations
- Set up automated testing for different OS versions and devices

